{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "6",
    "case_summary": "A 75-year-old male with diabetes reported experiencing dull pain in the upper abdomen for one week and observed swelling in the left upper abdomen. He did not have vomiting, fever, or gastrointestinal bleeding. His medical and family history were unremarkable, and he did not smoke or consume alcohol. Physical examination revealed a 14 × 10 cm mass in the epigastrium and left hypochondrium with minimal intrinsic mobility. Routine biochemical tests were normal. Imaging studies, including an ultrasonogram and CT scan of the abdomen, identified a large heterogeneous mass measuring 13 × 10 cm, extending from the tail of the pancreas to the anterior pararenal space, lesser sac, and gastrosplenic ligament, enveloping the posterior aspect of the fundus, body, and greater curvature of the stomach. One day post-admission, the palpable mass size reduced to 8 × 6 cm without worsening symptoms. A repeat ultrasonography showed the mass size decreased to 8 × 8 cm. Upper endoscopy revealed a large bulge in the fundus and corpus of the stomach posteriorly, with an opening in the posterior part of the corpus, edematous margins, and the release of serous fluid and necrotic material into the stomach. Fluid analysis was normal for CEA and CA 19-9.\n\nBiopsy taken from the edge of the opening showed bundles of spindle cells with elongated nuclei and tumor cells and was strongly positive for CD117 immunohistochemical examination.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
